                    {
                        "sense_id": "758b7cb9-84d7-3562-bba4-d9bf73cbac24",
                        "entry_id": "2016db4e-7545-3131-be6e-abc5eb572400",
                        "glosses": [
                            "La force divine, une des parties de l’âme humaine."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Mythologie égyptienne"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "fe3dbea0-dfa2-47d7-a2e5-408048654e65",
                                "sense_id": "758b7cb9-84d7-3562-bba4-d9bf73cbac24",
                                "text": "À côté du corps il y a ce que J. Vandier appelle « les principes spirituels » (5): akh, ba, ka, khaibit, ren, śẖm (6). On est d’autant plus puissant, semble-t-il, que les âmes sont nombreuses ; et on peut même, à cet effet, avoir plusieurs âmes de la même catégorie. L’akh, force divine attribuée originellement aux dieux et au roi qui leur est assimilé, fut très tôt accordée à tous les hommes. Le ba, représenté par un oiseau (cigogne ?) de même que l’akh (ibis), et traduit en général […]"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "83e16861-f830-444c-b423-cef21c386930",
                        "source_entry_id": "2016db4e-7545-3131-be6e-abc5eb572400",
                        "source_sense_id": "758b7cb9-84d7-3562-bba4-d9bf73cbac24",
                        "target_lang_word": "akh",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "akh",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "98b93f9e-8dab-3f77-aad0-0c5be503e0b4",
                                        "entry_id": "f02753c9-9bc0-338c-b949-6c24dcef84bc",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "In Egyptian mythology, (roughly) a spirit of the dead that has successfully completed its transition to the afterlife."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a0ce71a6-e857-4f13-82f6-9b43a3bbcdfa",
                                                "sense_id": "98b93f9e-8dab-3f77-aad0-0c5be503e0b4",
                                                "text": "Their abode is heaven; and the Akh, by contrast with the Ba, does not retain any relation to the body.… It is a deceased, a transcendent being, without earthly or material ties; and, as such, it is the most spiritualized of the various concepts of the dead."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2f07f3f1-eed4-4242-9dfc-7cbc2c85a283",
                                                "sense_id": "98b93f9e-8dab-3f77-aad0-0c5be503e0b4",
                                                "text": "After spending the night asleep in their tombs, the akhs would wake each morning at sunrise and “come forth from the necropolis” to enjoy an ideal life, free from the cares of physical existence."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "01790ff1-5bfa-43d1-b272-19f8f1f38391",
                                                "sense_id": "98b93f9e-8dab-3f77-aad0-0c5be503e0b4",
                                                "text": "The akh would later become the state achieved when the ba and ka are rejoined."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "52fb9d56-4a30-47a9-b896-bcf66acfc83a",
                                                "sense_id": "98b93f9e-8dab-3f77-aad0-0c5be503e0b4",
                                                "text": "If a person’s ka and ba were not reunited and akh failed to develop, then everlasting life would not occur."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4d1fea28-a684-4e2a-a612-0cb890d7769f",
                                                "sense_id": "98b93f9e-8dab-3f77-aad0-0c5be503e0b4",
                                                "text": "Different postmortem aspects of the individual are mentioned in ritual texts, so that it is unclear how they relate to one another: the ka (what leaves the body when death occurs), the ba (the personality of the individual), and the akh (a glorified bodily form)."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
